00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.680
So to start off with, what is
serverless in Google Cloud?

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.090
So our definition of
serverless in Google Cloud

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.750
is to build and run software
without provisioning,

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.990
managing, or maintaining
physical or virtual machines,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.850
completely easing the
pain of the users.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.953
Just give us your code,
and we'll run it for you.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.370
Furthermore, to
elaborate on that,

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.100
we consider that to be an
operating model, zero server

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.350
management.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.750
We consider it to be
completely secure.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.710
And pay for only what you use,
in terms of a billing model.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.700
Additionally, we want
developer experience

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.310
and a programming
model focused around

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.580
service-based management.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.040
So you build your
own microservices

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.200
that you deploy in the Cloud.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.910
We want it to be event-driven.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:57.800
So you submit a image
to a GCS bucket,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.262
and you trigger
a Cloud Function,

00:01:59.262 --> 00:02:01.220
or any other event--
submit a Pub/Sub message--

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.340
or any other activity that
you have to trigger sprinkles

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.030
of computer code in the Cloud.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.020
Lastly, we want it to be open
with zero vendor lock-in.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.060
And these are the two
paradigms around which

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.920
we define serverless,
an operating

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.520
model and a programming model.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.770
And we have what we call
a full stack serverless

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:26.250
offering in Google Cloud,
spread across seven areas.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.810
A bunch of compute offerings--
but over broad other areas

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.730
such as storage, DevOps, data
analytics, messaging, ML &amp; AI,

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.770
smart and chatbots.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.200
A lot of these technologies
are decade long

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.060
of Google engineering
experience exposed to you.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.342
For example, BigQuery
is like a big engine

00:02:44.342 --> 00:02:46.050
that we are working
on for several years.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.630
And we exposed that as a
serverless offering for you.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.340
In this talk, we're
going to jump deeper

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.420
into some of the compute
offerings, especially, and also

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.085
the Java offerings
in this space.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.893
So to start off
with-- you know, we

00:03:01.893 --> 00:03:03.560
want to talk about
Cloud Functions which

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:08.010
is, in compute, a source-based
event driven functions.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.135
A simple example
would be like you

00:03:10.135 --> 00:03:11.760
submit an image to
Google Cloud Storage

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.510
and you want some
work done on it.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.288
So you can trigger a bunch
of APIs to do that work.

00:03:17.288 --> 00:03:18.830
After Cloud Functions,
the next thing

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.380
in the spectrum we talk
about is App Engine.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.595
App Engine is our decades-long
old serverless offering.

00:03:24.595 --> 00:03:25.970
If you want to
build a simple web

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.140
app in your favorite
programming language,

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.030
with stateless,
serverless offerings,

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.408
we consider App Engine
as something for that.

00:03:34.408 --> 00:03:35.950
And then we have
our latest offering,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.100
which is Cloud Run, which
is serverless containers

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.460
for fully managed
HTTP request response,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.010
which can be run on a fully
managed system within Google

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.530
Cloud, as well as on
Kunernetes engine.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.533
So dig deeper into Cloud
Functions specifically.

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:54.950
As I mentioned
before, we consider

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.380
Cloud Functions as our
event-driven serverless

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.380
compute platform.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.760
What we envision it as
triggering sprinkles

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.280
of computer code,
which basically then

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.940
help you build your applications
of gluing together code

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.020
around your radius services.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.090
For example, you have
different Cloud services

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:13.520
that could even invoke
Cloud Functions that

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.640
invoke different APIs,
the result of which

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.420
is then processed
by these services.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.050
As I said, upload an
image to Cloud Storage,

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.240
do some work using an ML
API, the result of which

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:25.550
could then be stored in
Google Cloud Storage as well.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:29.730
It's a fully managed offering.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.530
It scales automatically.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.980
And it's pay per use.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:36.418
We consider it to be a
streamlined development model,

00:04:36.418 --> 00:04:38.210
where you write your
function and deploy it

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:41.260
with a single command.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.690
And it has integration for
events with different GCP

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:48.780
products, as well as HTTP.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:52.890
Classic use cases
for Cloud Functions

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.130
could be like webhooks.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:59.100
You know, you have different
clients that invoke a webhook,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.050
and then that could
start triggering a HTTP

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.700
function to process some work.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.220
You can invoke different
APIs to a Cloud Function,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:11.050
such as the ML API and so on.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.870
And events-- events
are very imperative.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.730
For example, a good
use case for this

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.260
could be like if
you had BigQuery

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.663
and you had a lot of audit
logs that you wanted to track,

00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:22.330
and you went through
logging accurately,

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.420
such as with Stackdriver,
which files events,

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.670
you could then use functions
to process those events

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:31.320
and do some analytics on that.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.240
The next use case would
be Lightweight ETL.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.902
For example, you upload
certain data to Cloud Storage,

00:05:37.902 --> 00:05:39.610
and then you can
trigger a Cloud Function

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.860
to do some processing
on that data,

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.600
and then store the
analysis in a data store.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.948
And lastly, iOT
and edge devices--

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.740
if you have different
edge devices deployed

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.900
across all the world
regions, like if you're

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.430
an oil company or
any other company,

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:58.420
those events could be
fired into the Cloud

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.230
maybe through a
messaging system,

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.680
and then you invoke a
function to do some processing

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.310
to store the results.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.220
So these broad
range of use cases

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.243
can be accommodated
under Cloud Functions,

00:06:08.243 --> 00:06:09.910
or function as a
service, as we call it.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.890
Currently, Cloud Functions is
offered in these run times.

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.300
They're all GA.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.660
In these same run times, we
have additional versions,

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.460
which are beta and alpha.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.560
And today, we are really
excited to announce

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:25.655
Java on Cloud Functions.

