Groovy co-founder.

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:38.530
So that's why I'm biased.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:41.310
And I like this
language on the JVM.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:42.310
So it's pretty simple.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:45.810
It's just a Groovy
script similarly

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.300
to what we had with Java 8.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.600
We just have the process
method here-- no need for class

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.460
or anything surrounding this.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.310
And same thing-- I'm
getting the writer.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:57.020
And I'm writing on the output.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:58.920
And I just wanted
to show you as well,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:05.130
since we did the deployment
earlier with the console,

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.720
the web console.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.360
You can also deploy
it from there

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:14.040
with G Cloud on the command line
with G Cloud Functions Deploy,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.690
the name of my function.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:18.900
I specify that I want to
use the Java 8 runtime,

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:20.670
even if it's another language.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:21.690
That's OK.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.850
HTTP trigger-- and
the entry-point,

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.170
that's actually main.

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:28.890
That's the name of my
Groovy script and process,

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:30.268
the name of the method.

00:13:30.268 --> 00:13:32.310
And I'm doing I'm doing
a deployment from source.

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:35.250
So I'm saying that's
the source, dot.

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:37.710
I can also do that for a Jar.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.620
So if I have a fat Jar
already pre-compiled,

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:43.560
I can also deploy it from there.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:48.120
And then just to prove that
