00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:58.290
there's going to be another
asynchronous event notifying

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:01.740
another Java function, which
will retrieve all the pictures

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:06.089
and update some metadata in
my Cloud Firestore database.

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:09.150
And also because I'm using
Firebase on the front end,

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.550
I'm able to update
the front end as soon

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:15.690
as I gather all the URLs
of the pictures that

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.109
have been processed.

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:21.660
So what I'd like to show you
is one of the Java functions--

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.980
the one doing the
image processing,

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:29.520
which is a Pub/Sub function, and
the one doing the cloud storage

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:30.720
event handling.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:34.440
So let's have a look
at the code here.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.980
So this is my image processor.

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:42.010
I'm going to hide that
to have a bit more space.

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:47.070
So this function is going to
receive a Pub/Sub message.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:49.530
So that's my process
method, the Pub/Sub message.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.800
Actually, under the hood,
we're using the GSON library,

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:56.310
which does marshaling,
unmarshaling of JSON

00:17:56.310 --> 00:18:00.130
to and from Java objects.

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:02.100
And if you go at
the very bottom,

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:07.410
actually there's my Pub/Sub
class here that's unmarshaled.

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:09.300
And I'm interested
in the data field

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:14.130
because this data field contains
my image request payload

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:16.830
with the file name,
message, et cetera.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:20.070
So I'm receiving that.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:22.140
I also have access to--

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.280
it's an optional parameter.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.970
There's a context if I need some
additional data about the event

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:30.185
ID, timestamp, event
type, resource, et cetera.

00:18:30.185 --> 00:18:31.560
I'm not using it
in the function,

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:37.110
but I will need to show
this context class.

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:42.690
Then I'm just decoding the
data from the Pub/Sub message,

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.840
and there, I'm using
JSON directly because I

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.700
know that what's in the
payload of the Pub/Sub message

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:49.950
is actually some JSON content.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.190
So I want to get an
instance of my image request

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:56.240
which contains the
file name, et cetera.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:02.170
I'm doing a bit of
cloud storage handling

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:04.710
because I want to retrieve--

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:05.210
where is it?

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:07.380
Yeah, image download--
I want to retrieve

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:10.290
the image from cloud storage.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:12.630
That's where I store
all my pictures.

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:14.800
And then there's a
long part of the code--

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:16.730
you're not allowed to
look at it too closely--

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:21.930
where I'm actually using Java
2D to do the image generation.

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:27.880
And when I go towards the end,
once I've drawn my picture,

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:31.650
I'm going to write that
and store that again

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.240
in another bucket
in cloud storage.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:38.740
So that's what I'm
doing there, actually.

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:43.080
OK, so this new processed
image is in cloud storage,

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.050
so it's going to invoke,
asynchronously, another

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:49.590
function which I call
the watchdog, which

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:55.300
is going to update Firestore
with this new information.

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:58.440
So similarly, I have
a GCS event that's

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:01.410
actually myself who
created and defined

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:03.420
this class at the bottom.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:06.240
I just used the bucket
and the name fields,

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.190
but there are other
additional attributes,

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.050
but I'm just interested
in those two.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:15.120
So I just create a simple
class with those two fields.

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:20.810
And then, here, I'm going to
use Firestore to specify--

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.750
so I'm doing some
regex stuff to retrieve

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.660
the name of the runtime
and things like that.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:29.640
And once I have
that metadata, I'm

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.700
going to put the information
in the Firestore.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:35.520
So I've got my
document reference,

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:40.230
and then I'm going to, here,
set the data in Firestore.

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:45.570
And then that's what is going,
later on, to update the UI here

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:46.830
with--

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:51.900
so if I do another little
test with some random message,

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:53.940
once the data is
stored in Firestore,

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.790
it's updating the
pictures down below.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:21:03.000
So that's about it for
functions in Java--

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:09.630
HTTP functions, background
functions with cloud storage,

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:11.320
and Pub/Sub.

