00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:06.110
which is also a very
lightweight framework, not based

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:10.640
on servlets, which is
using the NETI library.

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:12.770
So I define my server.

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:15.710
I say, I'm listening
on port 8080.

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:17.960
I'm serving some static
content, for example,

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:20.120
of all the pictures
of the hexagons.

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:24.200
And then I've got two routes.

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:27.290
So I have one for displaying
the pictures, the hexagons.

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.210
And the other one
which is the one which

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:33.200
generates the sudokus.

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:37.380
And I'm passing
a path parameter,

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:39.500
which is the level here.

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.180
And what I wanted
to do is to use

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:47.720
this Java 12 experimental
switch expression notation.

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:49.470
So notice that it's a
little bit different

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:52.210
compared to the old
switch case statement.

00:27:52.210 --> 00:27:54.180
So you can specify
different values,

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:56.010
like easy and cool
on the same line,

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:58.680
and you're using an arrow
instead of the columns.

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:00.480
And you don't have
to use the break.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.690
So for that, I really
needed to use Java 12.

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:05.970
Of course, I could have
used an if/else statement,

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:09.120
but just because
I can, I did it.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.270
So I have this switch
statement, and I also

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:16.380
wanted to show here that I
am using the process builder

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:19.890
to invoke my sudoku generator.

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:22.780
So that's what I'm doing here.

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:25.140
I'm starting the process,
and I'm going to retrieve

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:28.570
the output of this
C, C++ program.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:33.240
And let me show you what my
build and my Docker container

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:34.290
look like.

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:36.920
So as you can see in my build--

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:38.970
I'm using Gretel for the build.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:43.680
As you can see, I specify
that I want to use the Java 12

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:45.310
syntax level.

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:47.280
But when I'm
compiling my code, I'm

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:52.320
also using this special
Java compiler flag

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:57.760
Enable Preview, which enables
this new experimental syntax.

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:58.260
What else?

00:28:58.260 --> 00:28:58.980
That's about it.

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:04.260
Here, I'm also using the shadow
plugging, which is doing--

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:09.540
which creates fat JARs when
you use Gretel for the build.

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:10.930
And the docker file.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:12.660
Let's have a look
at the docker file.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:18.280
So it's a multi-stage
docker build.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:20.430
I actually have the
first step, which

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:25.440
is about building my application
and invoking the shadow JAR

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.870
command to create my fat JAR.

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:31.620
So this is this part
building my app.

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:33.710
But then, here's the
image I'm going to use

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:36.360
and that I'm going to
push on the cloud run.

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.880
So I needed-- oops, sorry.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:43.050
I needed my little
binary file here

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.480
for generating the sudokus.

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:49.500
And from the first
stage of my build,

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:51.900
I'm retrieving the fat JAR.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:53.660
And then what else?

00:29:53.660 --> 00:29:55.710
I'm exposing port 8080.

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:58.710
And then I can specify
the entry point as well,

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:01.350
and specify, OK,
I'm launching a JAR

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:04.380
and I'm enabling this
preview feature that

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:08.040
should be available when
I'm running my code.

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:09.700
So if you look at
the very bottom,

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:13.730
you can see how I build with
Docker and then Docker Run.

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:16.300
So I can run that locally
without a problem.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:21.480
And then I must have somewhere
a docker push command.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:22.950
Well, somewhere in my history.

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:24.720
It's too far.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:30.030
So I can very easily,
again, push my own--

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:33.090
whichever version of Java that
I want to use in my application.

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:35.020
Here I'm using Java 12.

00:30:35.020 --> 00:30:36.640
And I can use, of course--

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:38.910
anything that can be put
in a Docker container,

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:41.410
basically, I can put that there.

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:44.550
And then I have my
service running here,

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:49.650
my sudoku-generator service
running in the cloud run cloud

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:51.330
console.

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:53.340
Yes, that's about
