00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:13.770
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN: So
switching back to the slides

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:15.270
as we move on with
our talk, we want

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:18.550
to talk about Serverless
Beyond Compute.

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:21.370
And specifically, the thing that
we wanted to talk about here

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:24.970
was compute is
not just a vacuum.

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:28.330
You need ancillary products to
work with serverless offerings

00:31:28.330 --> 00:31:30.280
to build an end-to-end service.

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:32.998
For example, let's say you
want to run a Cloud Function,

00:31:32.998 --> 00:31:34.540
but you want to run
it on a schedule,

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:38.320
like every Friday morning
or so on, to do some work.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:41.740
Or you want to queue a bunch of
tasks or a bunch of functions

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:43.900
to be executed at a
later point in time--

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:46.830
a month later, a
week later, or so on,

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:48.790
or you want some
retry semantics.

00:31:48.790 --> 00:31:51.880
Then you need some kind of
queue to queue those works

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:54.800
to various microservices.

00:31:54.800 --> 00:31:58.190
Or if you want to store your
end results in a data store,

00:31:58.190 --> 00:32:01.450
you need a data store where you
can store the result for, say,

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:04.620
your ML work being processed
from a Cloud Function.

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:06.300
So given that, I'm
going to talk to you

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:09.540
about two specific products
that could help in building

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:12.070
these end-to-end applications.

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:14.160
The first is Cloud Tasks.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.680
Cloud Tasks is a
point-to-point queuing system

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:20.520
for asynchronous tasks
across microservices.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:23.990
It's a queuing product for
reliable task offloading.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:26.130
It's a fully managed service.

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:28.530
If you have any
long-running async work,

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:31.200
such as you have
a job that needs

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:34.170
to be run to process
an image and the end

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:36.520
result of the processing
needs to be stored somewhere,

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.490
you can queue it up in
this particular product.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:42.690
It has really good rate
limits and retry controls.

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:44.670
In queuing systems,
it's very important,

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:47.640
if you have a
workflow of systems,

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:51.780
that you set the limit to the
weakest link in the chain.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.540
Cloud Tasks really
gives the end users

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:58.110
a control to set these rate
limits and retry semantics.

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:00.810
So based on the guarantee
needs of your system,

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:03.360
you can tweak it accordingly.

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:06.360
Further, it provides
for flexible routing

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:08.010
with HTTP Auth.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:11.490
So you can hit your end
points and services securely

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:16.360
within Google Cloud, as well
as your on-prem systems.

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:20.890
To know more about Cloud
Tasks and its retry semantics,

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:25.080
its rate limits, dynamic
scheduling, and other features,

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:27.400
we have a dedicated
session for this

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:30.000
called Cloud Tasks,
Point-to-Point Asynchronous

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.880
Tasks Across
Microservices tomorrow.

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:34.855
We really appreciate it
if you can come there.

00:33:34.855 --> 00:33:36.480
It's an exciting
product and the team's

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:37.563
worked really hard for it.

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:43.810
Moving on, as you're
building the systems,

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:46.100
we always need a Cron system.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.910
For example, if you're
scheduling functions

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:51.130
every Friday, you need
something that you

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:52.870
can set your schedules on.

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:56.530
And at Google Cloud, we have a
product called Cloud Scheduler.

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:59.380
It's what we call
Cron for the Cloud.

00:33:59.380 --> 00:34:02.020
It's a reliable
enterprise-grade Cron scheduler

00:34:02.020 --> 00:34:03.890
that can reach
different endpoints,

00:34:03.890 --> 00:34:07.060
such as HTTP, Pub/Sub,
and App Engine targets.

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:10.840
It's fully managed and
pay for what you use.

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:14.949
It supports the popular
Unix Cron syntax

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:17.230
and has a delightful
UI experience,

00:34:17.230 --> 00:34:20.590
so you can create your
schedules from the UI, as well

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:21.610
as the CLI.

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:24.880
Furthermore, you have a really
good monitoring dashboard,

00:34:24.880 --> 00:34:28.060
so you can track the status
of your jobs through the UI

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:31.806
and also take corrective
action wherever necessary.

00:34:31.806 --> 00:34:33.389
This is something
that is very common.

00:34:33.389 --> 00:34:35.579
We have seen a lot
of customers use

00:34:35.579 --> 00:34:38.900
Cloud Functions coupled with
Cloud Scheduler on a recurring

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:39.739
schedule.

00:34:39.739 --> 00:34:42.870
They really like the
Unix Cron syntax,

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:47.620
so we highly recommend trying
this product out as well.

00:34:47.620 --> 00:34:49.860
Additionally, it
offers security so you

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:51.989
can hit the different
endpoints with security

00:34:51.989 --> 00:34:55.055
and authentication both
within Google Cloud, as well

00:34:55.055 --> 00:34:56.055
as your on-prem systems.

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:02.250
To know more about
Cloud Scheduler,

